{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Educational Technology Evaluation","title":"Cost and Sustainability","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting cost and sustainability to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"04","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_cost_and_sustainability","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/05/03"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_5_4_cost_and_sustainability_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_5_4_cost_and_sustainability_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_5_4_cost_and_sustainability_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_5_4_cost_and_sustainability_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Cost and Sustainability\n\n![Cost and Sustainability textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/05-04-cost-and-sustainability.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe sticker price is the smallest number in an edtech decision and the only one most evaluations record. \"Free for educators\" hides a per-seat tier that triggers at student forty-one; a \\$1,200 license hides thirty hours of unpaid teacher setup; a generous trial hides a free tier that can vanish next budget cycle along with three years of student work. Cost is not the line on the invoice; it is the total a school actually pays over the time it actually uses the tool, in money and in teacher hours. Sustainability asks the next question: can the school keep paying that total, and can it get its data back if it stops?\n\nTeachers carry the hidden costs personally, in evenings spent configuring a platform no one budgeted to maintain. Naming the full cost of ownership and the sustainability cliff is how an evaluation respects both the budget and the people who run the tool.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Build a total cost of ownership across a realistic time window, including license tiers, training, and teacher time.\n- Translate \"free\" into its true price by finding the seat cap, the paid tier, and the labor it assumes.\n- Identify a sustainability cliff: a free tier that can be discontinued, a price that can rise, or an export gap that traps student data.\n- Document a decision in which the cheaper sticker price loses to the more sustainable total.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA department compares two platforms for 120 students over three years. Tool A lists at \\$1,200 per year with vendor-led training included. Tool B advertises \"free for educators,\" and the early instinct is \"pick B, it is free.\" But B's free tier caps at 40 students, so 120 students require the paid tier, and B offers no contractual guarantee that the free option, or the export of student work, will exist next year.\n\nThe decision worth recording is not \"we picked the free one.\" It is whether the department built the three-year total for both tools, including the teacher hours and the data-export risk, and let that total decide.\n\n## Core Concept\n\n**Total cost of ownership (TCO)** is the sum of every cost a tool imposes across the window you will use it, not the first-year license alone. For school technology it has three layers that the sticker price omits:\n\n- **Tiered licensing.** \"Free\" and per-seat prices change at thresholds. A free tier capped at 40 seats costs nothing until your 41st student, then jumps to the paid tier for the whole roster.\n- **Training and setup.** Vendor-led training may be bundled (a real saving) or absent, in which case the teacher absorbs the setup as unpaid labor. Thirty hours per teacher per year is a cost even when no invoice names it.\n- **Teacher time over the window.** Setup recurs; multiply per-year hours by the number of years and teachers.\n\n**Sustainability** is the separate question of whether the total can be sustained and exited:\n\n- **Price stability:** can the vendor raise the price or end the free tier?\n- **Data portability:** does the contract guarantee export of student work, including history, if you leave?\n\nA tool with a low sticker price but a discontinuable free tier and no data export sits on a **sustainability cliff**: cheap until the day it strands your students' work.\n\n**Recent Evidence.** The exit check has legal backing, not just prudence. Under FERPA's school-official exception, a school must retain direct control over student data held by a vendor, including the return or destruction of records when the contract ends, and the FTC's amended COPPA Rule (effective June 23, 2025, with full compliance required by April 22, 2026) bars vendors from retaining children's data longer than reasonably necessary and declined to exempt ed-tech operators from regulation. Getting your data back is a contractual obligation to negotiate up front, not a courtesy to hope for at exit.\n\nBuild the ledger yourself with the explorer. Set the roster, the years, the per-seat tier that triggers above the free cap, and the unpaid teacher hours. Watch the \"free\" tool's true total climb as soon as your roster crosses the seat cap and the labor is counted in dollars. The model assumes the free tier covers up to 40 seats; above that the whole roster moves to the paid per-seat tier.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"What \\\"Free for Educators\\\" Actually Costs Over the Window\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"students\",\"label\":\"Students on the roster\",\"min\":10,\"max\":300,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"students\",\"default\":120},{\"name\":\"years\",\"label\":\"Years you will use the tool\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"years\",\"default\":3},{\"name\":\"seatcost\",\"label\":\"Paid per-seat license once you exceed 40 free seats\",\"min\":0,\"max\":20,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"$/seat/yr\",\"default\":7.5},{\"name\":\"hours\",\"label\":\"Unpaid teacher setup per year\",\"min\":0,\"max\":60,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"hours\",\"default\":30},{\"name\":\"wage\",\"label\":\"Value of a teacher hour\",\"min\":0,\"max\":80,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"$/hr\",\"default\":40}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Licensing total over the window (paid tier on full roster once over 40 seats)\",\"unit\":\"$\",\"expr\":\"students * seatcost * years\"},{\"label\":\"Unpaid teacher labor over the window\",\"unit\":\"$\",\"expr\":\"hours * wage * years\"},{\"label\":\"True total cost of ownership (license + labor)\",\"unit\":\"$\",\"expr\":\"students * seatcost * years + hours * wage * years\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"students\",\"output\":2}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check question=\"Tool B is 'free for educators' but its free tier caps at 40 seats; you have 120 students for 3 years. A colleague says 'free is the lowest cost.' What is wrong with that claim?\" explanation=\"'Free' names the invoice, not the total. With 120 students you exceed the 40-seat cap, so the whole roster moves to the paid per-seat tier, and the setup still costs unpaid teacher hours every year. The true total includes the tier you triggered and the labor no line item names.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Nothing; if it says free, the lowest cost is free.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Free names the invoice, not the total; 120 students trigger the paid per-seat tier plus unpaid teacher hours.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is wrong only if the tool is bad.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThree course anchors keep the accounting honest. **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** asks whether the cost buys learning value or only features. **Critical edtech studies** (Selwyn, 2014) names the labor and lock-in vendors externalize onto schools and teachers. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** flags \"free\" tools whose real price is student data monetized elsewhere, the cost that never appears in dollars.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nStart with a thin claim that many students write on a first pass:\n\n> I made the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nThe stronger version treats the artifact as evidence: it names what changed and leaves a reader able to audit the decision.\n\n### Worked Example: From Sticker Price to Cost of Ownership\n\nScenario: a department compares two platforms for 120 students over a three-year window.\n\n1. **Sticker comparison (before).** Tool A lists at 1,200 dollars per year; Tool B advertises \"free for educators.\" Early note: \"Pick B, it is free.\"\n2. **Built the cost of ownership.** Tool A, three years: 3 x 1,200 = 3,600 dollars, with vendor-led training included. Tool B \"free\" tier caps at 40 students, so covering 120 students requires the paid tier at 900 dollars per year (2,700 over three years), plus about 30 hours of teacher setup each year that no one is paid extra to do.\n3. **Named the sustainability cliff.** Tool B's free tier could be discontinued; its export option does not include student work history. Tool A guarantees data export in its contract.\n4. **Annotated judgment call (after).** The revised brief reads: \"On dollars alone the gap is small (3,600 vs 2,700). Once 90 hours of unpaid teacher setup and the data-export gap are counted, Tool A is the more sustainable choice for our staffing. We rejected the 'free' framing of Tool B because it hid the per-seat tier and the teacher-time cost.\"\n\nThe decision is defensible because a reviewer can see every cost line, including the human time that the word free concealed.\n\nNow run the department's three-year, 120-student decision yourself. The scenario tests whether you let the word \"free\" decide or build the full ledger first.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Free Tool B versus paid Tool A for 120 students over three years\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"Tool A lists at \\\\$1,200 per year with training included. Tool B advertises \\\"free for educators.\\\" Your department serves 120 students for three years. What do you do first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Pick B; free beats \\\\$3,600 every time\",\"next\":\"free\",\"feedback\":\"You decided on the sticker, not the total.\"},{\"label\":\"Build the three-year cost of ownership for both\",\"next\":\"build\",\"feedback\":\"You refuse to let the invoice stand in for the total.\"}]},\"free\":{\"prompt\":\"You start setting up Tool B and discover its free tier caps at 40 students. What happens for your 120-student roster?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The whole roster moves to the paid tier at \\\\$900 per year\",\"next\":\"build\",\"feedback\":\"The ’free’ framing hid a per-seat tier; back to the ledger.\"}]},\"build\":{\"prompt\":\"Three-year math: Tool A is 3 x \\\\$1,200 = \\\\$3,600, training included, export guaranteed. Tool B paid tier is \\\\$900 per year = \\\\$2,700, plus about 30 unpaid teacher setup hours each year. On dollars alone Tool B is cheaper. What is still missing?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Nothing; \\\\$2,700 beats \\\\$3,600, pick B\",\"next\":\"missing\",\"feedback\":\"You stopped before counting labor and the exit terms.\"},{\"label\":\"90 hours of unpaid teacher time and the data-export terms\",\"next\":\"exit\",\"feedback\":\"You count the human time and the sustainability question.\"}]},\"missing\":{\"prompt\":\"You picked B on dollars, but you never counted the 90 hours of unpaid setup or checked whether you can get student work back. What two costs did ’cheaper’ hide?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Teacher labor and data portability on exit\",\"next\":\"exit\",\"feedback\":\"Now bring them into the ledger.\"}]},\"exit\":{\"prompt\":\"Tool B’s free tier can be discontinued and its export omits student work history; Tool A guarantees export by contract. With 90 hours of unpaid labor and a data-export gap counted, which is the more sustainable choice for this department?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Tool A, despite the higher sticker price\",\"next\":\"end_a\",\"feedback\":\"You let the full total and the sustainability terms decide.\"},{\"label\":\"Tool B, because the dollar figure is lower\",\"next\":\"end_b\",\"feedback\":\"You let the smallest number win after all.\"}]},\"end_a\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: on dollars the gap was small (\\\\$3,600 vs \\\\$2,700). Once 90 unpaid hours and the data-export gap are counted, Tool A is the more sustainable choice. The cheaper sticker lost to the more sustainable total.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the reversal the section teaches, with every line shown.\",\"choices\":[]},\"end_b\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: choosing on the dollar figure alone leaves 90 unpaid teacher hours uncounted and accepts a data cliff. The brief must name those costs, not let the smallest number win.\",\"feedback\":\"Reconsider: the more sustainable total, not the lowest sticker, should govern.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nUse the learning value first, the total cost of ownership second, and the sustainability terms third. A tool is affordable only when the school can pay the full multi-year total and recover its data on exit.\n\nWhen a price looks attractive, run it through three tests before it enters the matrix:\n\n1. **Tier check:** What threshold (seats, features, storage) moves this tool off its advertised price, and where does your roster sit relative to it?\n2. **Labor check:** How many teacher hours, per year and across the window, does setup and maintenance require, and is anyone paid for them?\n3. **Exit check:** Can the vendor raise the price or end the tier, and does the contract guarantee export of student work history if you leave?\n\nA tool that fails the exit check is not automatically rejected, but its sticker price cannot be trusted, and the brief must record the cliff.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is equating \"free\" or \"cheapest\" with \"lowest cost.\" Free names the invoice, not the total, and it routinely hides a seat cap or unpaid labor. The second is omitting teacher time because it has no line item; uncounted hours are still a cost, and they fall on the people least able to refuse them. The third is ignoring data portability until the day a school tries to leave and finds its students' work locked in.\n\nA defensible brief shows the full multi-year total for each tool, names the teacher hours explicitly, and records the exit terms beside the price.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nPut both tools on a three-year ledger. Tool A is arithmetic: 3 x \\$1,200 = \\$3,600, training included, export guaranteed. Tool B looks cheaper until you apply the tier check: 120 students exceed the 40-seat free cap, so the real price is the paid tier at \\$900 per year, \\$2,700 over three years. The dollar gap has already shrunk to \\$900.\n\nNow apply the labor check, the column the sticker price never shows. Tool B assumes about 30 hours of teacher setup each year, 90 hours across the window, paid by no one. Whether or not you convert those hours to dollars, they are a real cost that tilts the comparison, and the brief must name them rather than let them stay invisible.\n\nFinally apply the exit check. Tool B's free tier can be discontinued and its export omits student work history; Tool A guarantees export by contract. On dollars alone the tools are close; once labor and the data cliff are counted, the more sustainable choice is the one with the higher sticker price. Recording that reversal, with every line shown, is the discipline of cost evaluation.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a ledger note rather than a single price: one row for license across the window, one for training, one for teacher hours, and one for exit terms. The point is to make every hidden cost a visible line so the comparison cannot be won by the smallest, loudest number.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. What is the true multi-year total, after tiers and thresholds?\n2. How many teacher hours does the tool cost, and who pays for them?\n3. What event (price rise, tier end, export gap) could strand the school or its data?\n4. When does the cheaper sticker price lose to the more sustainable total, and can I show the lines that prove it?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nFor this section, the brief is strong when cost appears as a multi-year ledger with separate lines for license, training, teacher time, and exit terms, so a mentor can see no cost was hidden inside the word free. Treat a single sticker number as the least trustworthy figure in the brief.\n\nA useful closing line would sound like this: \"I changed the brief so cost is shown as a three-year ledger including 90 hours of teacher setup and the data-export gap. I kept out the suggestion to recommend Tool B as 'the free option' because that framing hid the per-seat tier, the labor, and the sustainability cliff.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same comparison to a one-teacher elementary classroom of 22 students. Now Tool B's 40-seat free tier actually covers the roster, so the licensing line genuinely is free, and the labor line shrinks because one teacher configures one section. The tool that lost at department scale may win here. But the exit check does not change: the free tier can still be discontinued and the export still omits history, so the sustainability cliff transfers even when the price does not. Use the shift to confirm that cost is recomputed per context while sustainability terms travel with the contract.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/05-04-cost-and-sustainability-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"05.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 05.04. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation\",\"cat531_supplement_cost_and_sustainability\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_cost_and_sustainability\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm the brief shows a multi-year total cost of ownership with separate lines for license tiers, training, and teacher hours, names the sustainability cliff (price stability and data export), and records any case where the cheaper sticker price lost to the more sustainable total.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning et al. (2024), Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Selwyn, N. (2014), Distrusting Educational Technology: Critical Questions for Changing Times (critical edtech studies). https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- Federal Trade Commission (2025), amended Children's Online Privacy Protection Rule (COPPA): data-retention limits and third-party-disclosure consent. https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2025/01/ftc-finalizes-changes-childrens-privacy-rule-limiting-companies-ability-monetize-kids-data\n- U.S. Department of Education, Student Privacy Policy Office, Responsibilities of third-party service providers under FERPA (Vendor FAQ). https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/sites/default/files/resource_document/file/Vendor%20FAQ.pdf\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_4_cost_and_sustainability_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_cost_and_sustainability","stem":"A tool advertises itself as 'free for teachers.' What does a defensible cost analysis still need to surface?","options":["Only the headline price, which is zero.","The full cost of ownership: training time, device or storage needs, paid upgrades for full features, and who pays when the free tier ends.","Whether the logo looks professional.","How many other teachers have downloaded it."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A zero sticker price is not the total cost. Sustainable adoption accounts for training time, infrastructure, feature-gated upgrades, and the risk that the free tier disappears.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_4_free_means_no_cost","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_cost_and_sustainability","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_4_cost_and_sustainability_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_cost_and_sustainability","stem":"Tool A costs 1,200 dollars per year. Tool B costs 600 dollars but needs about 30 hours of teacher setup each year. For a sustainability decision, why can the cheaper sticker price mislead?","options":["Sticker price is the only real cost.","Teacher time has a cost; if 30 hours of setup is worth more than the 600-dollar difference, Tool B is not actually cheaper to sustain.","Tool B is always better because it costs less per year.","Setup time is free because teachers are salaried."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Sustainability weighs recurring human time alongside dollars. Unpaid teacher labor is a real, finite resource, and ignoring it understates the true cost of the cheaper-looking option.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_4_sticker_price_is_total","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_cost_and_sustainability"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_4_cost_and_sustainability_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI assistant to estimate the multi-year cost of adopting a platform. It returns a tidy three-year total. How should you use it?","options":["Submit the AI total to the budget committee as the official figure.","Treat it as a draft model, then verify each assumption (price tier, renewal increases, headcount) against the real contract and local context.","Trust it because the math is internally consistent.","Use it only if it shows the tool is affordable."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An AI estimate is only as good as its assumptions, which may not match your contract or enrollment. Use it as scaffolding, then ground every input in the actual quote and local figures.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_4_ai_estimate_as_budget","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat531_supplement_cost_and_sustainability"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_4_cost_and_sustainability_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A grant covers a platform fully for one year. Which question most protects against an unsustainable adoption?","options":["Does the tool look good in year one?","What happens in year two when the grant ends: who pays, can we export our data, and what is the exit cost if we stop?","How many features does year one include?","Did students enjoy the first month?"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Sustainability is a multi-year question. Grant-funded year one can mask a cliff: the defensible brief plans for renewal cost, data portability, and the cost of leaving.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_4_year_one_funds_sustainability","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision","cat531_supplement_cost_and_sustainability"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"b8e2e65a652de05ca41267de334ad734bcfd1dd8fe43376f8f02ecacd2bb8030","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}